  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 256 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe extended forecast continues to feature increased rain chances\nover much of our region along with cooler temperatures. Weak\nridging aloft will be in place over our forecast area on Tuesday,\nbut the surface boundary will remain roughly oriented along the\nPecos River Valley. Scattered to locally numerous showers and\nthunderstorms should develop along this boundary again Tuesday\nafternoon. The upper-level ridge will shift eastward into eastern\nportions of Texas and Oklahoma by Tuesday evening as another\nupper-level trough lifts across the Great Basin. Medium range \nmodel solutions remain in agreement with bringing a slug of\ntropical moisture northward from Mexico into far west Texas and\nmuch of New Mexico between these features Tuesday night into\nWednesday. Deep moisture remains in place Wednesday night into\nThursday night underneath increasing southwesterly flow aloft. \nThis pattern will support a very good chance of showers and \nthunderstorms (>60%) over much of our western and northwestern CWA\nlate Tuesday through late Thursday, while other isolated to\nscattered showers and storms (20-50%) will be possible through\nthis period over much of the rest of the forecast area. The deep\nmoisture and persistent shower/thunderstorm potential may result\nin an increased potential for heavy rainfall and flash flooding\nover some of our western and northwestern counties, including the\nsoutheast New Mexico plains and the higher terrain areas of west \nTexas later this week. We will continue to monitor trends closely.\nMoist southwesterly flow aloft will persist into late week, so\nchances of showers and thunderstorms will continue through the\nlatter part of the extended forecast. Highs will trend cooler\nTuesday through the end of the week with values actually closer \nto what is normal for late September (generally in the 80s for\nmost, except mid to upper 70s in the higher terrain and 90s along\nthe Rio Grande). Overnight lows will also be cooler with readings\ngenerally in the 60s, except for 50s in the higher elevations and\nlower to mid 70s along the Rio Grande.",
